  "slug": "codon-associated-29a5",
  "summary": "Recent work links codon position, N-terminal sequence and regulatory layers to translation efficiency and protein stability across human and yeast systems.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that sequence features within coding regions, particularly codon position, are associated with translation behavior. The published POSCO study introduces a position-specific codon occurrence score linked to translational efficiency, tAI, ribosome occupancy and folding energy across the human transcriptome. A separate preprint trains a machine learning model to predict protein stability from N-terminal sequences of annotated and alternate isoforms, validates motifs experimentally, and applies the model to alternative isoforms. A yeast multi-omic preprint on glucose starvation examines cis-regulatory programs across multiple RNA regulatory layers, a theme less directly tied to codon-level mechanisms. What is new is the position-resolved codon metric applied at transcriptome scale, alongside sequence-based stability prediction for isoforms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-23"
}
